PRODUCT_RELEASE_PLAN_AR.md §4.1: "أنشئ API محلياً versioned (v1) فوق IPC
محلي فقط: named pipe مع ACL على Windows وUnix-domain socket مع permissions
على Linux. يصدر DTOs ولا يكشف اتصال SQLite للواجهة. لا يُفتح HTTP على LAN في
الإصدار الأول."

Added:
- internal/ipc/ - the local-only transport. Unix domain socket at mode 0600
  on Linux; a Windows named pipe on a protected DACL granting access to
  exactly the creating user's SID (via github.com/Microsoft/go-winio, a new
  dependency - the standard, widely-used library for this on Windows;
  Docker/containerd/Kubernetes all use it for the same purpose). Both
  implementations were run for real on their actual platform, not simulated:
  ipc_test.go + ipc_unix_test.go on Linux (checked the socket file's mode
  directly), ipc_test.go + ipc_windows_test.go on this real Windows machine
  (checked the SID lookup and that the constructed SDDL actually parses via
  winio.SddlToSecurityDescriptor).
- internal/api/v1/ - the versioned API itself: GET /v1/health, /v1/capabilities
  (the registry's live snapshot), /v1/events, /v1/incidents, with since/
  until/limit/kind/family/subject/rule/min_severity query parameters, a
  single typed JSON error shape, and empty results returned as [] rather
  than null. It never exposes store.Store or a database handle - every
  response is exactly what Store.Query/QueryIncidents already return,
  serialized the same way the CLI's own -o json output already is.
- TestServedOverRealLocalIPC (internal/api/v1/integration_test.go) proves the
  two packages actually work together: a real ipc.Listen() listener, a real
  http.Server bound to it, and a real http.Client dialing that exact
  transport (net.Dial("unix", ...) on Linux, winio.DialPipe on Windows) -
  not httptest.NewRecorder, which never touches internal/ipc at all. Run for
  real on this Windows machine: PASS.

Not yet done, and stated plainly rather than implied: the API is read-only
and does not yet have the authn/authz layer beyond what the transport itself
enforces (file mode / pipe ACL). Verifying a connecting peer's credentials
in-process (SO_PEERCRED on Linux, named-pipe client-process inspection on
Windows) as defense in depth beyond transport permissions is not built. The
CLI has not been migrated to use this API yet - it still reads the store
directly, which is untouched and still fully tested (see cmd/netrewind's own
test suite, unaffected by this change).

Verification: gofmt clean, go vet clean (native and GOOS=linux) on Windows;
the full repository test suite passes on both Windows and Linux, the latter
with -race.
